---
description: Prevent SQL injection vulnerabilities by using parameterized queries and prepared statements
alwaysApply: true
---

sql-injection-prevention:

- Always use parameterized queries or prepared statements when constructing SQL queries
- Never concatenate user input directly into SQL strings using template literals or string concatenation
- Use ORM/query builders that handle escaping automatically (e.g., Sequelize, TypeORM, Prisma)
- Validate and sanitize all user inputs before database operations
- Apply principle of least privilege to database users

Bad patterns to avoid:
```javascript
const query = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '${userInput}'`;
const sql = `DELETE FROM posts WHERE id = ${req.params.id}`;
```

Good patterns to use:
```javascript
db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = $1', [userInput]);
User.findOne({ where: { username: userInput } });
```
